{#
  Three columns, grouped by what somebody came for (owner, 2026-08-29). It was
  one run of fourteen links that wrapped wherever the window happened to end,
  so "Privacy" could sit beside "Get involved" and the grouping was whatever
  the browser decided that day.

  The groups, in the order somebody needs them:

    1. THE ATLAS        what to go and look at
    2. TAKE PART        how to put something in, or ask us something
    3. THE SMALL PRINT  licences, policies, the things you read once

  Then the colophon, then where to find us.

  Columns rather than rows, so each group is a shape the eye takes in at once
  instead of three lines that read as one paragraph. Each is a <nav> with its
  own heading, which is what makes the grouping real to a screen reader too
  and not only to somebody looking at it.
#}
<footer class="foot"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="footcols mono">
    <nav class="footcol" aria-label="{{ 'footer.col_atlas'|trans }}">
      <h2>{{ 'footer.col_atlas'|trans }}</h2>
      <a href="{{ path('regions') }}">{{ 'footer.regions'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('blog') }}">{{ 'nav.blog'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('changelog') }}">{{ 'footer.changelog'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('roadmap') }}">{{ 'footer.roadmap'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('pages') }}">{{ 'footer.all_pages'|trans }}</a>
    </nav>

    <nav class="footcol" aria-label="{{ 'footer.col_take_part'|trans }}">
      <h2>{{ 'footer.col_take_part'|trans }}</h2>
      <a href="{{ path('join') }}">{{ 'footer.get_involved'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('contributors') }}">{{ 'footer.contributors'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="https://wiki.cyclingcommons.org/">{{ 'footer.wiki'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="https://wiki.cyclingcommons.org/governance/">{{ 'footer.governance'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('contact') }}">{{ 'footer.contact'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('bug_report') }}">{{ 'footer.report_bug'|trans }}</a>
    </nav>

    <nav class="footcol" aria-label="{{ 'footer.col_small_print'|trans }}">
      <h2>{{ 'footer.col_small_print'|trans }}</h2>
      <a href="{{ path('accessibility') }}">{{ 'footer.accessibility'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('privacy') }}">{{ 'footer.privacy'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('terms') }}">{{ 'footer.terms'|trans }}</a>
      <a href="{{ path('credits') }}">{{ 'footer.credits'|trans }}</a>
      <span class="footby">{{ 'footer.stewarded_by'|trans }} <a href="https://bikecoders.life">BikeCoders</a></span>
      {# The build marker lands HERE and not wherever version.js finds a .mono
         first. Without a slot it appended itself to .footcols, which is the
         grid, so it opened a fourth column of its own beside the three real
         ones (owner, 2026-08-29). It belongs with the steward line: both say
         who and what made this, not where to click. #}
      <span class="footver" data-cc-version></span>
    </nav>
  </div>

  {# The colophon LAST: licences and ownership are what this is, not what
     somebody came to the footer to click. Its own class, because it sits
     further down than the gap between the link rows. #}
  <span class="mono colophon">{{ 'footer.rights'|trans }}</span>

  {#
    Where to find us, below the licences.

    GitHub moved OUT of the link rows and in here (owner, 2026-08-28): it is a
    place we are, not a page of this site, and a text link between "Governance"
    and "Get involved" read as the latter.

    ICONS, not words, because the row is a set of logos a reader recognises
    faster than any label, and because it stays one line in five languages.
    Every one carries a real accessible name, and `title` for a pointer, so the
    glyph is never the only thing saying what it is.

    `rel="me"` on each: it is what lets a Mastodon profile verify that this site
    claims that account back, and it costs nothing on the others.

    Bluesky stands in for X. Not a slot we are leaving empty out of neglect:
    an atlas built on open data has no business sending riders somewhere that
    is not (and Bluesky's AT Protocol is).

    Handles come from config/packages/support.yaml, so a channel that has no
    account yet simply does not render rather than linking to a 404.
  #}
  {% if social|default([]) is not empty %}
    <nav class="foot-social" aria-label="{{ 'footer.social_label'|trans }}">
      {% for s in social %}
        <a href="{{ s.url }}" rel="me noopener" target="_blank"
           title="{{ s.label }}" aria-label="{{ s.label }}">
          {% include 'partials/_social_icon.html.twig' with {name: s.name} only %}
        </a>
      {% endfor %}
    </nav>
  {% endif %}
</div></footer>
